<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="dimension-1" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Dimension 1: <custom ref="dim1-name"/></title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      This dimension is mentoring focused on students as individuals having a distinct culture and
      identity, and developing the activities, the teaching methods, the pace, and everything else
      based on how students are responding. Students, individually, drive everything else! To
      activate this, it is essential to value active participation and give voice to students,
      using a learn by doing approach to continuously improve our understanding of how students
      learn best, their strengths, and their interests.
    </p>

    <p>
      One of our programs' goals is to allow students to direct their own learning according to
      their skills and interests. Rather than having a set list of very specific objectives for
      the students to learn, the program activities are designed to have general objectives that
      can be satisfied in multiple ways. Students do not need to complete every single task, but
      rather the tasks are guidelines for how to scaffold the understanding <mdash/> students are
      free to explore the activities and end up doing one that is sufficiently challenging for them.
    </p>

    <p>
      At the same time, we do not want students to work totally alone <mdash/> we want students
      to balance working out their own answers with learning with other students. Not only does
      this reinforce teamwork and social skills, but this also has students sharing knowledge with
      each other. This has two benefits: (1) that students are encouraged to practice sharing and
      explaining their knowledge in a way that makes sense to other students, and (2) that students
      can learn from each other. This second one is important since mentors understand activities
      differently than the students, so the explanation that makes the most sense to a student may
      naturally come from another student. In this way we can maintain the focus on students by
      having them help each other rather than immediately having the mentor intervene.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsection xml:id="dim1-beliefs">
    <title>Mentors' Beliefs and Attitudes</title>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>I believe that I am not the only source of learning.</li>
        <li>I believe that students learn better if they are invested in the topic.</li>
        <li>I believe that students should have autonomy and agency.</li>
        <li>I believe that I can modify the session to better fit my students.</li>
        <li>I believe that students' explanations can help other students.</li>
        <li>I believe that I should not be <q>testing</q> my students for a significant part of the session.</li>
        <li>I believe that I can also learn from my students and this will improve my practice.</li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <p>Which other beliefs can shape your practice towards a student-centered teaching?</p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="dim1-components">
    <title>Components</title>

    <subsubsection xml:id="dim1-adaptability">
      <title>Component 1: <custom ref="dim1-comp1-name"/></title>
      <p><custom ref="dim1-comp1-desc"/></p>

      <paragraphs>
        <title>Mentors' Actions</title>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li>(Planning) Use your students' identities, cultures, and interests to design or
            adjust activities that feel relevant to them.</li>
            <li>(Planning) Prepare extra resources you can bring into the session (books, websites,
            manipulatives, guest speakers, etc.).</li>
            <li>Invite students to suggest examples or situations from their own lives to enrich
            the activity.</li>
            <li>Invite students to reshape or adapt parts of the activities to match their
            interests.</li>
            <li>Adjust the session flow and content in response to how students are engaging and
            making sense of the main ideas.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </paragraphs>

      <paragraphs>
        <title>Students' Perspectives</title>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li><q>The activities are about stuff I like.</q></li>
            <li><q>I can decide a little bit what I want to work on.</q></li>
            <li><q>We change things if we come up with a cooler idea.</q></li>
            <li><q>We often use things from our own lives in the activities.</q></li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </paragraphs>
    </subsubsection>

    <subsubsection xml:id="dim1-ongoing-guidance">
      <title>Component 2: <custom ref="dim1-comp2-name"/></title>
      <p><custom ref="dim1-comp2-desc"/></p>

      <paragraphs>
        <title>Mentors' Actions</title>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li>(Planning) Anticipate the different strategies and reasoning students might use to
            solve the tasks, including follow-up questions.</li>
            <li>Monitor all students' work, asking intentional follow-up questions that deepen
            understanding and address misconceptions.</li>
            <li>Create a safe environment where mistakes are addressed and used as opportunities to
            learn, and students are encouraged to explore and take risks.</li>
            <li>Identify students who can be challenged further or take on leadership roles, and
            empower them to do so.</li>
            <li>Identify students who are particularly struggling with the content, guiding them
            without fully isolating them from the rest of students, and helping them gain confidence
            by interacting with other students.</li>
            <li>Keep quick notes on students' progress to inform future sessions.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </paragraphs>

      <paragraphs>
        <title>Students' Interactions</title>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li><q>They help me figure things out without just giving me the answer.</q></li>
            <li><q>When I'm stuck, they ask me questions that make it click.</q></li>
            <li><q>They notice when I'm ready for something harder.</q></li>
            <li><q>They remember what I need help with for next time.</q></li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </paragraphs>
    </subsubsection>

    <subsubsection xml:id="dim1-interactions">
      <title>Component 3: <custom ref="dim1-comp3-name"/></title>
      <p><custom ref="dim1-comp3-desc"/></p>

      <paragraphs>
        <title>Mentors' Actions</title>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li>Give everyone sufficient time to think individually before group discussions,
            adjusting it as needed.</li>
            <li>Avoid having just very few students dominate the whole group discussion.</li>
            <li>Encourage peer-to-peer cooperation, conceptual discussions, and idea-sharing to
            solve problems.</li>
            <li>Redirect a question that a student asked you so that it is answered by another
            student.</li>
            <li>Give structured and guided chances for a student to comment on other students'
            work.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </paragraphs>

      <paragraphs>
        <title>Students' Perspectives</title>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li><q>I have time to think on my own.</q></li>
            <li><q>I help other students and they also help me.</q></li>
            <li><q>We figure things out together, not just by ourselves.</q></li>
            <li><q>I can share my ideas and ask questions without feeling dumb.</q></li>
            <li><q>People actually listen when I talk in my group.</q></li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </paragraphs>
    </subsubsection>

    <subsubsection xml:id="dim1-connections">
      <title>Component 4: <custom ref="dim1-comp4-name"/></title>
      <p><custom ref="dim1-comp4-desc"/></p>

      <paragraphs>
        <title>Mentors' Actions</title>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li>Prompt students to contrast their answers and to connect their approaches to those
            of their peers.</li>
            <li>Involve students in creating and summarizing the shared story of the ideas
            development, acknowledging each person's contribution.</li>
            <li>Highlight connections between mathematical concepts and students' personal
            experiences or community life.</li>
            <li>Use exit tickets, quick polls, sentence starters or oral reflections to capture
            student thinking and growth at the end of each session.</li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </paragraphs>

      <paragraphs>
        <title>Students' Perspectives</title>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li><q>I like hearing how other people solved it <mdash/> it gives me new ideas.</q></li>
            <li><q>We talk about how this connects to stuff we did before.</q></li>
            <li><q>I can explain how my way is like someone else's way.</q></li>
            <li><q>I feel like we all came to conclusions as a group, and that's great.</q></li>
            <li><q>It makes sense how this topic shows up in life.</q></li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </paragraphs>
    </subsubsection>

  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="dim1-activity">
    <title>Activity: Identifying Teaching Profiles</title>

    <p>
      Watch carefully the four short clips 1<ndash/>4 (in any order). For each, give it a short
      title and a short description (bullet points). If you are doing this activity with
      colleagues, discuss your choices. To close, reflect about the limitations or opportunities
      for improvement of each profile. Profile A has been done as an example.
    </p>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>Profile A: (example)</li>
        <li>Profile B:</li>
        <li>Profile C:</li>
        <li>Profile D:</li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <p>
      The goal of this activity is to be more aware of our own teaching habits, by seeing
      contrasting styles in teaching.
    </p>

